00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:26,220
 Sounds good.

214
00:09:26,220 --> 00:09:29,740
 For the next question, I'm sure I know the answer already to this,

215
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:32,940
 but I'm curious about how you guys articulate the reasoning.

216
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:38,220
 So I hear a lot, like, with AI making it easier than ever to build and replicate software right now,

217
00:09:38,220 --> 00:09:42,940
 right, is open source still worth it as a go-to-market strategy?

218
00:09:42,940 --> 00:09:44,380
 Like, why or why not?

219
00:09:48,140 --> 00:09:49,100
 Give it a second.

220
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:51,180
 I think it's just the one, one long press.

221
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:52,620
 And if it's on, yeah.

222
00:09:52,620 --> 00:09:55,660
 Oh, okay, that's, should have read the manual.

223
00:09:57,340 --> 00:09:59,100
 Yeah, I think it absolutely is.

224
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:04,540
 And I think, you know, there's trade-offs with open source, of course.

225
00:10:04,540 --> 00:10:10,780
 And there's, there's definitely, you know, the concerns about people forking the product and

226
00:10:10,780 --> 00:10:11,900
 things like that.

227
00:10:11,900 --> 00:10:16,220
 And if anything, I think those have all gone away at this point, because it doesn't matter.

228
00:10:16,220 --> 00:10:16,940
 Right?

229
00:10:16,940 --> 00:10:21,340
 It's like you can rebuild it and no one's, no one's forking it, no one's stealing your code.

230
00:10:21,340 --> 00:10:21,660
 Right?

231
00:10:22,780 --> 00:10:28,140
 But on the flip side, you still have all those upsides of it brings people in, people are more

232
00:10:28,140 --> 00:10:29,260
 comfortable with it.

233
00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:34,780
 There's advantages on the security side, advantages on the community side.

234
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:38,540
 So I think, yeah, absolutely, it's, it's still very important.

235
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:44,140
 Yeah, I think for us, we have, it's a trust thing, right?

236
00:10:44,140 --> 00:10:46,620
 I think very few people self-host PostHog.

237
00:10:47,740 --> 00:10:49,900
 But people like the fact that they can.

238
00:10:51,180 --> 00:10:54,220
 And that actually increases the trust in our cloud product.

239
00:10:55,980 --> 00:10:57,500
 You know, so that I think is really important.

240
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:01,500
 It also just immediately signals that your product is meant for developers, right?

241
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:02,940
 It's meant for technical people.

242
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:09,020
 You know, one of the things that our customers really appreciate is like, when we do a fix,

243
00:11:09,020 --> 00:11:12,780
 we literally just like, give them a link to the pull request showing the fix, you know,

244
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:16,860
 and stuff like that, that I think, again, makes a lot of sense if you're building an engineering tool.

245
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:20,780
 Now, I think I often talk to founders who are not building engineering tools.

246
00:11:20,780 --> 00:11:22,700
 I think the benefit is obviously much, much less.

247
00:11:23,900 --> 00:11:24,460
 Yeah, exactly.

248
00:11:24,460 --> 00:11:29,180
