249
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:30,860
 We're open core.

250
00:11:30,860 --> 00:11:33,740
 And what that means is like, we have a kernel, first of all, we started with the kernel.

251
00:11:34,380 --> 00:11:39,900
 We built that first, it was a signal for people of like the technical prowess of us,

252
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:43,980
 because, you know, we're systems researchers, you know, is a mix of C and assembly.

253
00:11:44,780 --> 00:11:46,380
 So it's, you know, much harder to do.

254
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:50,860
 And so it was kind of for folks like, okay, this is a badge, these guys know what they're talking about.

255
00:11:51,580 --> 00:11:54,220
 That's still true, you know, the kernel's there.

256
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:57,820
 We built now around it like a cloud platform that can use this.

257
00:11:57,820 --> 00:12:00,940
 It can also use Linux, it can use Windows actually these days.

258
00:12:00,940 --> 00:12:04,940
 So you can use it as a traditional, like fast-performing cloud.

259
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:07,660
 But for us, there's the question of actually security.

260
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:09,660
 It's a guard, it's an API endpoint.

261
00:12:09,660 --> 00:12:16,540
 And if everything else was open source, people could just like dive in and like potentially compromise

262
00:12:16,540 --> 00:12:20,540
 our cloud platform or the cloud platform of our customers as well.

263
00:12:20,540 --> 00:12:23,260
 So there's this, this is one aspect.

264
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:28,220
 But I, you know, it's a balancing act there, because I think there's equal parts adversarial,

265
00:12:28,220 --> 00:12:31,020
 and there's equal parts like all the fixes that are coming along, right?

266
00:12:31,020 --> 00:12:31,980
 It's two sides of the game.

267
00:12:31,980 --> 00:12:36,140
 And every time there's something bad that comes in terms of like the CVEs from Linux kernel or whatever,

268
00:12:36,140 --> 00:12:38,140
 there's a fix that immediately comes afterwards.

269
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:40,220
 So like we're in a like a steady state, right?

270
00:12:40,220 --> 00:12:40,860
 Between the two.

271
00:12:41,820 --> 00:12:43,820
 And so it really just depends.

272
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:46,140
 It's an operational thing at the end of the day.

273
00:12:46,140 --> 00:12:47,500
 Like what do you believe is best?

274
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:48,780
 Yeah, 100%.

275
00:12:48,780 --> 00:12:53,580
 I think as a developer myself and all my startup friends, I think trust, you know, that's a big thing.

276
00:12:53,580 --> 00:12:58,220
 When we see something that's open source, I think the community always really get behind it.

277
00:12:58,220 --> 00:13:01,580
 And I think an interesting one I heard about like recruiting too, you know, you get a lot of great

278
00:13:01,580 --> 00:13:05,900
 talent because it's open source, people get, you know, involved and interested in that, right?

279
00:13:05,900 --> 00:13:07,420
 So that was my spicier question.

280
00:13:07,420 --> 00:13:11,100
 But now I also want to turn it back to you guys to get some hot takes.

281
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:17,100
 So what is the most, what do you believe that the market, what do you believe like the most

282
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:22,380
 conventional wisdom about open source and go to market that you actually disagree with?

283
00:13:22,380 --> 00:13:25,420
 That's like you hear all the time on Twitter, you see it and it's just,

284
00:13:25,420 --> 00:13:29,020
 you just strongly disagree with about open source and GTM.

285
00:13:34,540 --> 00:13:39,740
 One that I used to hear more is that the value of open source is getting contributions.

286
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:46,700
 And actually, that might be true for some types of software, we've never had like really substantial

287
00:13:46,700 --> 00:13:48,220
 contributions from the community.

288
00:13:48,220 --> 00:13:51,020
 But yeah, for us, it's always been more about feedback.

289
00:13:51,020 --> 00:13:54,300
 Like we'd rather have the feedback and we implement it than like, you know,

290
00:13:54,300 --> 00:13:56,220
 other people can implement stuff in our software.

291
00:13:59,020 --> 00:14:00,300
 In terms of go to market.

292
00:14:03,500 --> 00:14:06,780
 Well, okay, so it's one interesting, and it's actually interesting that you're open core,

293
00:14:06,780 --> 00:14:09,420
 because we tried open core and it really didn't work for us.

294
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:17,820
 And it didn't work because we would sell, you know, features in theory, like unlocking the kind of,

295
00:14:17,820 --> 00:14:20,620
 you know, stuff that isn't the open source bit.

296
00:14:20,620 --> 00:14:27,740
 And then really what customers think they're buying is like 24/7 on-call SRE support to make sure that,

297
00:14:27,740 --> 00:14:33,820
 like, you know, postdoc works in every weird iteration of like, you know, infrastructure that people could come up with.

298
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:41,020
 At one point, we were literally debugging people's setups of like, through like pictures taken of a screen,

299
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:46,380
 because they're like, you know, policies were so strict that they weren't allowed to, you know,

300
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:47,100
 take screenshots.

301
00:14:47,100 --> 00:14:48,140
 So it's like really painful.

302
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:52,620
 And I know now people do more like BYAC where they, you know, I guess maybe that's what you do,

303
00:14:52,620 --> 00:14:57,020
 but where you kind of bring in, you know, you control it, but you're sort of in an account.

304
00:14:57,820 --> 00:14:58,620
 So that might work better.

305
00:14:58,620 --> 00:15:01,900
 But, you know, that I think was very hard for us to make work.

306
00:15:01,900 --> 00:15:06,380
 And actually, when we just pivoted to only focusing on cloud for monetization, that went way better.

307
00:15:06,380 --> 00:15:09,100
 We literally then just like gave away the open source for free.

308
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:14,700
 But yeah, that still generated kind of word of mouth and usage for our cloud as well.

309
00:15:14,700 --> 00:15:20,220
 I think definitely the most valuable thing we can get from open source is issues more than pull requests.

310
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:22,460
 And I think this has been true even before AI.

311
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:24,780
 There was a book on building in the open.

312
00:15:24,780 --> 00:15:30,540
 It's one of the Stripe press books, and it shows like a power curve in terms of like contributors to any open source project.

313
00:15:30,540 --> 00:15:39,580
 And even recently, the creator of OpenClaw, he was telling, they don't actually accept pull requests anymore.

314
00:15:40,300 --> 00:15:44,780
 They just accept issues because an issue is content, is a prompt basically to fix something.

315
00:15:44,780 --> 00:15:48,460
 And that's a user actually going through the effort of typing out what they run into.

316
00:15:49,100 --> 00:15:51,820
 So this is very, I think, very, it could be continuing.

317
00:15:51,820 --> 00:15:56,060
 And you also, if you think about like where we're going, you know, we're building software factories.

318
00:15:57,580 --> 00:16:05,900
 We trust more the factory that we've built and all the rules that we've applied rather than some random person that's coming along and like just, I don't know, vibe coding like a solution.

319
00:16:05,900 --> 00:16:10,460
 Like it's much harder to know whether that's correct, even if you might have like substantial tests.

320
00:16:12,060 --> 00:16:18,220
 But then on like on the whole GTM front, I think, I mean, GTM engineering has like blown up in terms of a whole like new space.

321
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:22,860
 I don't think even the term GTM engineered like existed, what, six months ago properly.

322
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:27,580
 And now there's like GTM meetups, GTM engineer meetups, and everyone's like sharing like techniques.

323
00:16:27,580 --> 00:16:31,820
 And I think that's because there's a big power that people are getting in terms of like changing role.

324
00:16:31,820 --> 00:16:35,020
 And we're all like architects and accessing more parts of the company, whatever.

325
00:16:36,220 --> 00:16:37,260
 It's definitely interesting space.

326
00:16:37,260 --> 00:16:45,100
 I mean, obviously automation, but at the same time, I think I don't like getting AI slop in my inbox.

327
00:16:45,100 --> 00:16:46,380
 Like I don't think anybody does.

328
00:16:46,380 --> 00:16:46,620
 Right.

329
00:16:47,180 --> 00:17:01,500
 And you can even if you have like remove the EM dashes or whatever, like, you know, we actually care more about like having handwritten, personal, whatever, like moments like moments like this, where I can talk with real people is so much more important than getting whatever my inbox.

330
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:08,700
 And my inbox, I swear since this morning, a thousand emails of like bullshit and like, yeah, that's not going to scale.

331
00:17:08,700 --> 00:17:09,980
 It's going to continue to grow like that.

332
00:17:09,980 --> 00:17:16,620
 So I think it's actually more important to have like physical in present events than actually all this outbound stuff.

333
00:17:19,100 --> 00:17:24,700
 Yeah, I could not agree more about the unfortunate state of pull requests and contributions.

334
00:17:25,740 --> 00:17:28,300
 I think they were more valuable in the past.

335
00:17:28,300 --> 00:17:30,620
 That's where maybe my opinion differs a bit.

336
00:17:30,620 --> 00:17:34,940
 But but yeah, that that value has unfortunately just tanked at this point.

337
00:17:34,940 --> 00:17:38,860
 It's all just AI and it just it's such a burden to deal with.

338
00:17:39,420 --> 00:17:49,740
 I like the approach of, you know, issues are more important because, yeah, at the end of the day, it's you know, there's it's a year.

339
00:17:49,740 --> 00:17:59,020
 I'm of multiple minds about it because it's I really like seeing the community PR as we've hired great engineers that were contributors from the community.

340
00:17:59,980 --> 00:18:08,300
 But then at the same time, it's like sometimes just the effort of even seeing if there's a PR there is lower than just fixing it.

341
00:18:08,300 --> 00:18:14,940
 And I mean, we we merge PRs that are internal all the time that there was a similar community PR that we find a week later.

342
00:18:14,940 --> 00:18:17,020
 And it's like, oh, man, that was there.

343
00:18:17,020 --> 00:18:20,780
 So it's just so hard to deal with because it's there's such an influx.

344
00:18:20,780 --> 00:18:24,300
 And so much of it is just one shot AI.

345
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:29,420
 And, you know, like you said, it's we have our own software factory and our own way to review this stuff.

346
00:18:29,420 --> 00:18:30,940
 And we really needed to go through that.

347
00:18:32,940 --> 00:18:35,420
 And on the go to market part of the question.

348
00:18:38,700 --> 00:18:39,260
 I don't know.

349
00:18:39,260 --> 00:18:42,700
 I don't know that I really have a take that I disagree with there.

350
00:18:43,500 --> 00:18:45,820
 Yeah, kind of following on the question.

351
00:18:45,820 --> 00:18:50,460
 I'm very curious what's the whole pull request part because I can't imagine it's such a it's definitely a big issue.

352
00:18:50,460 --> 00:18:54,140
 I can't imagine since, you know, with AI now, how many more pull requests you're getting.

353
00:18:56,380 --> 00:18:59,660
 How do you guys kind of see the movement going with open source, right?

354
00:18:59,660 --> 00:19:05,100
 Do you think that there won't be pull requests anymore where it would actually just be like people submitting issues?

355
00:19:06,140 --> 00:19:07,260
 Or is there a middle ground there?

356
00:19:07,260 --> 00:19:09,580
 Because I do think the recruiting part is very valuable.

357
00:19:09,580 --> 00:19:12,060
 Having people contribute, they kind of buy in on the project, right?

358
00:19:12,060 --> 00:19:14,540
 That's why a lot of folks really love open source.

359
00:19:14,780 --> 00:19:18,940
 How are you guys going to see open source as a movement, you know, going forward as well?

360
00:19:20,700 --> 00:19:23,340
 I think there's a big question about maintainability.

361
00:19:24,540 --> 00:19:30,460
 It's not free to maintain software, both in terms of human hours to look at it previously.

362
00:19:30,460 --> 00:19:32,460
 And now with AI, it literally costs you something.

363
00:19:32,460 --> 00:19:41,740
 I think even with the latest like TypeScript or like Rust rewrite, that was a whole section in the Cloudflare blog post about who's going to maintain this, right?

364
00:19:41,740 --> 00:19:46,460
 Because now there's two and now, you know, oh, thank you, Theo and, you know, and T3 chat and whatever.

365
00:19:46,460 --> 00:19:48,220
 And that's all going to be like help with that.

366
00:19:48,220 --> 00:19:52,620
 But does that mean we're going to get to a point where we're actually having like converging software?

367
00:19:53,420 --> 00:19:55,500
 Are we going to have like, you know, this like staples?

368
00:19:55,500 --> 00:20:00,060
 We had like staples before, but does that mean we're going to continue to have that?

369
00:20:00,060 --> 00:20:02,140
 And like people are going to trend towards certain ideas.

370
00:20:02,140 --> 00:20:04,780
 Is, for example, everything going to be rewritten in Rust?

371
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:10,380
 And in the case of like whatever tool that you're using, it kind of falls into a specific category.

372
00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:15,420
 And then like it just becomes part of a foundation that like generally starts to happen a lot.

373
00:20:15,420 --> 00:20:18,780
 But at the same time, we're going to have, I think we're going to have way more personal software.

374
00:20:18,780 --> 00:20:23,500
 It doesn't have to be like people are just going to throw something out there and it's going to be for them.

375
00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:24,860
 And they don't care about maintenance.

376
00:20:24,860 --> 00:20:27,180
 It's just, you know, I have a bunch of tools that I use.

377
00:20:27,180 --> 00:20:30,140
 I don't care about having people come along and fix bugs.

378
00:20:30,140 --> 00:20:31,100
 If you fix a bug, great.

379
00:20:31,100 --> 00:20:34,380
 I'm not going to accept it probably, right?

380
00:20:34,380 --> 00:20:35,420
 Just because it's my software.

381
00:20:39,260 --> 00:20:42,860
 Yeah, I think there's a lot.

382
00:20:42,860 --> 00:20:47,740
 I think it'll converge on for certain things.

383
00:20:48,780 --> 00:20:56,300
 Using similar libraries, known battle-tested software is going to return to being more important.

384
00:20:56,300 --> 00:21:00,780
 And there are certain things that we're just going to figure out it's a bad idea for everyone to be rewriting.

385
00:21:01,340 --> 00:21:02,780
 Linux kernel being one of them.

386
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:09,580
 You'd just rather like all be kind of working in the same direction and on a stable base there.

387
00:21:09,580 --> 00:21:12,700
 You know, there's different opinions on how it should work.

388
00:21:14,140 --> 00:21:18,620
 But it's, you know, you want to know that you're building from a solid foundation there.

389
00:21:18,620 --> 00:21:20,780
 And then there's other things that will be more personal.

390
00:21:20,780 --> 00:21:26,300
 And it will be, yeah, it's just as easy to rewrite it as it is to contribute to what's there.

391
00:21:26,300 --> 00:21:31,100
 And, you know, libraries are going this way as well, where it used to be you had to find the

392
00:21:31,100 --> 00:21:37,740
 right library to bring in for a thing because you weren't going to create your own code diff visualizer.

393
00:21:37,740 --> 00:21:42,940
 But now it's like, no, maybe you should just build the code diff visualizer because it'll be what you want.

394
00:21:42,940 --> 00:21:47,260
 And, you know, pulling in libraries is not risk-free anymore.

395
00:21:47,260 --> 00:21:49,100
 We're all learning more and more every day.

396
00:21:49,820 --> 00:21:51,500
 So there's there's a balance there.

397
00:21:51,500 --> 00:21:52,780
 Cool, perfect.

398
00:21:52,780 --> 00:21:53,980
 Yeah, we're nearly at time.

399
00:21:53,980 --> 00:21:57,980
 So I'll ask one more question about who to market regarding open source.

400
00:21:57,980 --> 00:22:01,420
 So we talked about how did you guys all get started, right?

401
00:22:01,420 --> 00:22:02,780
 But obviously times change.

402
00:22:02,780 --> 00:22:09,180
 So in this day and age, with this current age right now, with everything going on, like,

403
00:22:09,180 --> 00:22:12,620
 if you were trying to get your first 100 users, what would you do?

404
00:22:12,620 --> 00:22:14,460
 Is there a method, a channel you would go with?

405
00:22:14,460 --> 00:22:15,900
 Or is there a philosophy you want to share?

406
00:22:15,900 --> 00:22:17,900
 Let's say launch fast, launch over and over again.

407
00:22:18,460 --> 00:22:21,260
 Like one piece of advice for the audience about go to market with open source.

408
00:22:24,140 --> 00:22:31,660
 It's maybe not even like go to market, but I was giving a talk yesterday to a sort of full hacker house.

409
00:22:32,780 --> 00:22:41,260
 And one of the things I said was, look, if you can build something in a day with Claude,

410
00:22:41,260 --> 00:22:43,420
 like, why would anyone else pay you for that, right?

411
00:22:43,420 --> 00:22:45,660
 Like, they can do the same thing.

412
00:22:45,660 --> 00:22:48,780
 The bar is like much, much higher now for like good software.

413
00:22:49,980 --> 00:22:54,300
 Or software that's valuable enough for people to buy it from you instead of from other people.

414
00:22:54,300 --> 00:22:59,980
 So, you know, I do think like, you know, a modicum of sort of effort or like, you know,

415
00:22:59,980 --> 00:23:05,180
 how defensible is this thing, I think, has raised the bar for what kind of software

416
00:23:05,180 --> 00:23:08,700
 actually makes sense to build anymore and what software makes sense to kind of try to sell.

417
00:23:10,140 --> 00:23:12,700
 I think distribution is a moat.

418
00:23:12,700 --> 00:23:16,220
 And I think it is gotten much harder.

419
00:23:16,220 --> 00:23:18,140
 Like, I think the internet has gotten much noisier.

420
00:23:18,140 --> 00:23:20,780
 And so you have to find something that makes you stand out.

421
00:23:20,780 --> 00:23:27,740
 And again, maybe it's blogs, maybe it's, you know, Twitter, maybe it's something else.

422
00:23:28,380 --> 00:23:30,220
 Maybe it's the crazy branding stuff that we do.

423
00:23:31,260 --> 00:23:33,660
 There's kind of a reason why that stuff works, right?

424
00:23:33,660 --> 00:23:35,340
 Because it stands out.

425
00:23:35,340 --> 00:23:38,620
 And so I think if you're trying to be early, you're trying to, you know,

426
00:23:38,620 --> 00:23:41,340
 you have to find a hook that kind of makes sense and it's different.

427
00:23:41,980 --> 00:23:46,780
 And then the last thing I think makes sense, I see a lot of people do now is, you know,

428
00:23:46,780 --> 00:23:49,740
 I think it used to be that you could just build some software, put it out there,

429
00:23:49,740 --> 00:23:50,780
 and people would use it.

430
00:23:50,780 --> 00:23:53,820
 I think now, you know, if you can actually solve people's problems, right?

431
00:23:53,820 --> 00:23:55,260
 That's way, way more valuable.

432
00:23:55,260 --> 00:23:58,700
 So can you be like a forward-deployed engineer that just solves problems for people?

433
00:23:59,580 --> 00:24:03,100
 Yeah, I think that ends up actually being a way that I see a lot of YC companies now.

434
00:24:03,100 --> 00:24:04,940
 Get the first couple of customers.

435
00:24:04,940 --> 00:24:06,220
 Very interesting.

436
00:24:06,220 --> 00:24:08,220
 And I'm sure we'll see the post-op buses.

437
00:24:10,300 --> 00:24:11,100
 You may be entitled.

438
00:24:11,100 --> 00:24:11,580
 Yeah.

439
00:24:11,580 --> 00:24:12,460
 Yeah, yeah.

440
00:24:12,460 --> 00:24:16,860
 I think one way I like to look at it is like AI is obviously on the trend up.

441
00:24:17,980 --> 00:24:22,620
 What gives more value the longer you're out there with AI?

442
00:24:22,620 --> 00:24:28,780
 Like do your customers, will they get more empowered using your product as AI gets better?

443
00:24:28,780 --> 00:24:31,340
 It's probably a good way to like look at how you're building something.

444
00:24:31,340 --> 00:24:34,780
 Because if they can get more value out of it over time, they're going to stick with it.

445
00:24:36,220 --> 00:24:37,420
 Obviously, being unconventional.

446
00:24:37,420 --> 00:24:43,580
 I think we live in a town where you see these types of ads and it's not just unique to you actually.

447
00:24:44,540 --> 00:24:47,660
 Everyone's putting memes on buses or stands or whatever.

448
00:24:47,660 --> 00:24:52,620
 So like even going beyond that, like I've seen people like put projectors up and spontaneously do things.

449
00:24:53,660 --> 00:24:56,940
 I even got like a floppy disk earlier as a card.

450
00:24:56,940 --> 00:24:58,220
 This is like actually amazing.

451
00:24:58,220 --> 00:25:01,740
 So shout out to ShipVox who gave me this.

452
00:25:03,180 --> 00:25:08,220
 And so definitely on this side, I think attention is very important.

453
00:25:08,220 --> 00:25:12,300
 I think being able to like get out there very quickly and big.

454
00:25:12,940 --> 00:25:16,460
 And so now you're seeing a lot of like big rounds.

455
00:25:17,260 --> 00:25:19,980
 There's a question of like things like Series A or Series AI.

456
00:25:20,780 --> 00:25:27,580
 And it's basically do you raise a fuck ton of money and like just basically try and get as much attention on you as possible.

457
00:25:28,700 --> 00:25:31,580
 And that's that's kind of a weird world to live in actually.

458
00:25:32,780 --> 00:25:33,420
 So, yeah.

459
00:25:36,940 --> 00:25:43,100
 Yeah, I think with open source, the most important thing is is figuring out that line of value of, you know,

460
00:25:43,100 --> 00:25:45,980
 people are going to use it for free and that's part of the point.

461
00:25:45,980 --> 00:25:52,220
 But then we're we're do you know they're serious and where do you know they're willing to pay for it?

462
00:25:52,620 --> 00:25:56,060
 So I think you've both got great approaches with theirs.

463
00:25:56,060 --> 00:25:59,500
 It's open source, but yet it's all about the service aspect.

464
00:25:59,900 --> 00:26:02,620
 And you really want to you can run it yourself.

465
00:26:02,620 --> 00:26:03,900
 Do you really want to?

466
00:26:03,900 --> 00:26:05,180
